      ⍝ Regression: Bugs25 #6 (Blake McBride). NumericCell::bif_binomial()
      ⍝ picks its ISO case table row from the raw (pre-rounding)
      ⍝ floating-point signs of A and B, but the actual K33_binomial()
      ⍝ call further down uses the ⎕CT-tolerant *rounded* K/N. For B
      ⍝ very close to zero from the negative side (e.g. ¯1E¯300), the
      ⍝ raw sign says "negative" (picks the row-3/row-6 negative-
      ⍝ binomial identity) while the rounded value is genuinely 0, so
      ⍝ K33_binomial()'s own Assert(N>=K)/Assert(K>=0) preconditions --
      ⍝ which assume the row and the rounded K/N agree -- aborted the
      ⍝ interpreter instead of returning a result. Fixed in
      ⍝ NumericCell.cc by reclassifying the row from the same rounded
      ⍝ K/N the identities use.
      ⍝
      ⍝ Bugs31 #15 (Blake McBride) update: 1!¯1E¯300 and 2!¯1E¯20 used
      ⍝ to return exactly 0 here (via the '0' row short-circuit's own
      ⍝ N==0 snap, the same fixed-INTEGER_TOLERANCE snap-to-zero bug
      ⍝ #15 fixed elsewhere) -- but N is only NEAR 0, not exactly 0, and
      ⍝ the true Gamma-based value is a tiny nonzero number (matching
      ⍝ the exact product-formula identity N×(N-1)÷2! for K=2, and the
      ⍝ exact N×(N-1)!/(N-1)!=N identity for K=1). Both now correctly
      ⍝ fall through to real_binomial() instead of snapping; the
      ⍝ original Bugs25 #6 concern (K33_binomial()'s Assert() aborting
      ⍝ the interpreter on a row/rounded-K-N mismatch) remains fixed --
      ⍝ this path no longer reaches K33_binomial() at all for these two.
      ⍝
